# tlo: budowa_tartaku
# rozdzial: Chapter Five
# tytul: The Sawmill
After that spring, time began to flow faster, as though someone had slackened its reins.
That very summer Urbaniak’s men carted the first wagonloads of beams to the stream. They built all through the autumn—hammering from morning till night, digging a race for the waterwheel and cursing the clay that caked their boots up to the knees. Children from the whole village came running to watch the great shed rise, while their elders shook their heads and said no good would come of it. # pokaz: budowa
Winters passed one after another. Jaromir went to school in Bukowiec for three more years. He learned to write so neatly that the teacher gave him carols to copy out for the organist, and to reckon so quickly that his father began taking him along to fairs to make sure the dealers did not cheat at the scales.{ ekwipunek ? ksiega: Mr Ignacy’s book he read so often that he knew half the legends by heart, and the rest he copied out, to be safe, into an exercise book of thick grey paper.}
Every spring the same marsh marigolds bloomed in the meadows, every summer the storks clattered on the smithy roof, and each autumn the smoke from the potato-haulm bonfires drifted over the fields just as it had when Jaromir went to school for the first time. Only one thing changed: year by year there was less birdsong from the direction of the forest, and more of the rasp of the saw. # pokaz: lata
Antek had shot up into a thin, serious lad who liked nothing better than to sit in the smithy with Hanka’s father, learning to forge horseshoes. Zosia, his little sister, now ran about the farmyard and got under the grown-ups’ feet{ polnocnica == uslyszala_krzyk:—pale and sickly, with a grey spot on her forehead that her mother hid beneath a headscarf}. Franek grew fastest of all four, and when he turned sixteen he was the first to go to the sawmill to earn his keep. A year earlier, on Midsummer Night, Kaśka, the Wójciks’ daughter, had vanished by the lake—Kaśka, who since harvest had followed Franek step for step. Franek mocked her in front of the lads until the very last day—and after that, never again.
And Hanka… Hanka was simply there. Every day, from morning till evening, right beside him—and Jaromir only began to notice it when once she was gone, for she had driven with her father to the fair in town, and the whole day long he could find no peace. # pokaz: hanka # dalej
-> tartak_stoi

= tartak_stoi
# tlo: tartak
Ever since Mr Urbaniak’s sawmill had gone up beside the stream that ran down from the forest into the lake, no one in Zapadlina fell asleep in silence any more. The great wooden shed had a waterwheel that turned day and night, and a saw whose grinding carried across the whole valley. From dawn to dusk wagons loaded with trunks rolled along the road beneath the forest, and on the yard beside it stacks of planks piled up, smelling of resin so strongly that you could catch it as far as Bukowiec.
The village split into two camps. Some went to work at the sawmill and came back with wages such as had never been seen in Zapadlina before. The others spat over their shoulders whenever a timber wagon passed them, and said quietly that the woods give nothing for free.
{ figurka_u_staruchy:
    His father belonged to the second camp. When Urbaniak sent a man round to talk him into it, he showed the fellow out beyond the fence without a word.
- else:
    His father wavered a long time. The money would have come in handy—a new plough, a cow, a roof over the barn—but every evening he sat on the doorstep listening to the saw, and his face grew grimmer and grimmer.
}
Jaromir had turned thirteen. He had finished school, shot up like a young spruce, and with every month he went out into the fields with his father more often.{ ekwipunek ? szczeniak: {imie_psa} had grown into a big dog with pointed ears and never left Jaromir’s side—only he could not be dragged towards the forest for all the world.}
Franek, who was seventeen now and had shoulders like a woodcutter, had been working at the sawmill for a year. One evening he came to Jaromir’s fence, jingling the coins in his pocket. # pokaz: franek
“Urbaniak’s looking for lads to stack planks,” he said. “Pays in silver, every Saturday. Come with me. Over the summer you’ll earn more than your father does in a year.”
