# tlo: ognisko_tartak
~ ekwipunek += monety
The work was hard. From first light Jaromir hauled planks until his hands were pricked all over with splinters and his hair was full of sawdust. The waterwheel creaked, the blade howled, and the water in the stream ran brown with bark. Everything smelled of fresh resin and wet timber, and his throat was raw with wood dust that he could not cough up even at night.
In the evenings, by the fire, the sawyers’ tongues loosened. Sparks shot into the darkness, and in the red glow the men’s faces looked older than they were. Old Wawrzyniec, who had worked at the frame saw all his life, said that something was not right at this sawmill. The saw had snapped twice already, though it was new. Of a morning the stacks of planks would lie scattered across the yard, and yet there had been no wind. And once, when he had stayed the night to guard the timber, he heard someone walking among the trunks in the dark, stopping at each one and sighing—long and heavily, like a woman over a grave on All Souls’ Day. # pokaz: stukanie
“The woods will have their due,” he kept saying, tossing a stick of kindling into the fire. “They always do.”
He told how, when the first people came down into this valley, the woods would not have them. Only when they set up wayside shrines along the forest and promised never to cross the Old Oak with an axe did the forest draw back. But nothing comes for nothing: once in a generation the woods take a child for a few nights. Of that Wawrzyniec spoke only once, in a lowered voice, and spat over his shoulder straight after. “That’s not a thing to be spread about. Who speaks of it, asks for it.”
Another time he said, “Wayside shrines are like nails in a fence. Pull out one, and the fence still stands. Pull out the lot…”
On the first Saturday Jaromir received a handful of coins from Urbaniak—heavy, cold, with an eagle on one side. Never before had he held so much money in his hand at once.
Hanka, when he showed her the coins, did not even touch them. “They shine nicely. I wonder how many trees went for them,” she said, and for several weeks she passed him on the road without a word, her chin in the air.
