# tlo: pole_orka
The summer slipped by for him behind scythe and plough. From the field he watched more and more wagons of trunks go by on the road beneath the forest, and every so often from deep in the woods came a crack—long, tearing—and then a dull crash that made the earth shudder. Each time, his father straightened up over the plough, took off his cap and crossed himself—just as he did when a coffin was carried through the village. # pokaz: drzewo
Franek called ever more rarely—at the sawyers’ fire he had new companions now, and when he did come, he talked of nothing but silver. News from the sawmill came by way of Antek, who carried his brother’s dinner to him every day, tied up in a cloth: that the saw had snapped twice already, though it was new, and that of a morning the stacks of planks lay scattered all over the yard, though the watchman swore he had seen no one. “Franek laughs at it,” Antek said. “Only he laughs too loud.” # pokaz: franek
Even before the haymaking his father led to Bukowiec the piglet that Mother had been fattening for the winter—there was nothing else to pay the tax with. He came back with an empty halter and did not utter a word till evening. Jaromir knew that if he had been hauling planks for Urbaniak, the piglet would still be squealing in the sty.
