# tlo: stary_dab
In the middle of June Urbaniak’s men reached the Old Oak.
It grew at the edge of the forest, where the path from Zapadlina turned towards Beech Ridge—so thick that four men could not have circled it with their arms, with a crown beneath which whole herds sheltered in the heat. It was said to have stood here back when there was not yet a single cottage in the valley. No one had ever broken so much as a twig from it. The bark was rough and warm as the hide of a sleeping animal, and its boughs rustled ceaselessly, even when not one blade of grass stirred in the meadow.
Now a fresh mark showed white on the bark. # pokaz: znak
{ ekwipunek ? ksiega:
    Jaromir remembered this oak from Mr Ignacy’s book. “The Old Oak at the edge of the forest,” the teacher had written, “is a tree the Lord of the Forest guards like his own heart. While it stands, the forest ends where it ends. Whoever fells it will open the Lord of the Forest a road into the village.”
- else:
    Jaromir recalled what Mr Ignacy had once told them on a Friday: that there are trees the Lord of the Forest guards like his own heart, and that the oldest of them mark the boundary beyond which his power ends.
}
“Monday we bring it down,” said Franek as they walked back together from the stream. “Urbaniak says a trunk like that’ll give planks enough for a whole manor house. The first cut’s fallen to me. Three days’ pay in advance.” # pokaz: chlopcy
# pytanie: Will Jaromir warn Franek?
*   [Warn Franek not to cut the Old Oak.]
    Jaromir caught him by the sleeve.
    “Don’t do it. That oak is the boundary. If it falls, the forest will come into the village.”
    Franek burst out laughing, but the laugh soon died when he looked his friend in the eyes. For a long time he said nothing.
    “You’re talking nonsense,” he muttered at last.
    All the same, on Monday morning he did not turn up at the sawmill. Urbaniak dismissed him that very day. # dalej
