# tlo: uboze_piec
That night nobody in the cottage slept soundly.{ ekwipunek ? szczeniak: {imie_psa} growled at the door until dawn.} The pots rang by themselves on the shelf, and in the chimney something scuffled and squeaked like a mouse.
Jaromir went down to the main room for water. It smelled of sourdough from the kneading trough, and the clay floor was cold under his bare feet. By the stove, in a streak of moonlight, sat a tiny old man—no bigger than a cat, in a patched coat belted with string. His grey beard reached right down to the ground, and its end wound around his thin little chicken legs. He had a big nose, bushy brows and narrow little eyes that most likely laughed as a rule, and on his head a red, pointed cap with a drooping tip. In his little hands he clutched a single stalk pulled from the straw mattress, and he was trembling all over, though embers still glowed in the hearth. # pokaz: uboze
“I’m afraid,” he squeaked in a thin little voice, before the boy could cry out. “It’s coming. Can’t you hear? It’s coming.”
Jaromir remembered what { figurka_u_staruchy && los_psa == oswojony: his grandmother | his mother } had once said about hearth sprites: that they live behind the stove in old cottages, that whoever feeds one prospers in house and farmyard, and whoever drives one off brings want upon the house. But she had also said, laughing, that it was a tale for children, to keep them from poking in the ashes—and Jaromir had never known which of the two she believed.
