The sawmill worked on as though nothing had happened.
{ franek_scial_dab:
    Franek got three days’ pay and a new coat from Urbaniak, but ever since he had felled the oak he was not himself. He slept by day and at night wandered by the lake. He said he could not sleep because something was calling him—in a thin, singsong voice, like girls at the washing. # pokaz: franek_noc
- else:
    Franek did not go back to the sawmill. He hired himself out to work other people’s fields, but he went about sullen and irritable, as if he regretted that money.
}
Strange things began to happen in the village. In the mornings people found clumps of moss between the vegetable beds that had grown overnight—soft and wet, as from beneath the oldest spruces. Milk soured in the jugs before it had time to cool, and every morning the green from the forest side stood a little closer to the fences, though no one saw it creep up. # pokaz: slady
Mr Urbaniak boasted at the inn in Bukowiec that within a year he would cut a road clean through the forest, and that the wayside shrines standing along it he would have moved, because they got in the way of the wagons.
Harvest was drawing near. # dalej
